<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="(-)-4&apos;-demethyl-epipodophyllotoxin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-8959 Podophyllotoxin| is a non-alkaloid toxin lignan extracted from the roots and rhizomes of |FRAME: TAX-1461176| species. It is used without modifications as a topical treatment of external warts, including genital warts caused by some types of the human papillomavirus (HPV). However, its main importance comes from semisynthetic derivatives that are used in a variety of medical applications as purgative, vesicant, antirheumatic, antiviral, and antitumor agents. These derivatives include |FRAME:CPD-16754|, |FRAME:CPD-16755| and |FRAME:CPD-17609|, which are used in various chemotherapies, including lung cancer, lymphomas, and genital tumors. These compounds induce DNA breakage through inhibition of topoisomerase II. |FRAME: CPD-8959 Podophyllotoxin| is produced from |FRAME: CONIFERYL-ALCOHOL| via a complex pathway, which is described in MetaCyc in three parts - |FRAME: PWY-5466|, |FRAME: PWY-7748| and |FRAME: PWY-7749|. While the upper portion of the pathway has been well understood since the 1990s, the lower part of the pathway was only discovered in 2015 |CITS: [26359402]|. To find the missing enzymes, the scientists looked for specific types of enzymes (methyltransferases, cytochrome P450 hydroxylases, oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenases and polyphenol oxidases) by analyzing RNA sequencing data from the |FRAME:CPD-8959|-producer |FRAME: TAX-93608|, looking for genes that are highly expressed in rhizome, stem, and leaf tissues (the typical expression pattern of genes involved in |FRAME: CPD-8959| biosynthesis) |CITS: [26359402]|. Candidate enzymes were tested initially by |FRAME: TAX-357|-mediated transient expression in |FRAME: TAX-4100|. Combinatorial expression was accomplished by coinfiltrating multiple |FRAME: TAX-357| strains, each harboring a different expression construct. Since the host did not produce sufficient amounts of the starting compound |FRAME: CPD-8912|, this compound was also infiltrated into leaves expressing the different constructs. Using this technique, the researchers were able to identify sixnew enzymatic steps and to synthesize |FRAME: CPD-17595|, |FRAME: CPD-8958| and even |FRAME: CPD-18757|, the |FRAME: CPD-16754| aglycone, in the tobacco plant |CITS: [26359402]|. An enzyme that produces |FRAME: CPD-8959| has not been discovered. The intermediate |FRAME: CPD-17595| is converted to |FRAME: CPD-8958| by an oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase, which catalyzes the closing of the central six-membered ring in the aryltetralin scaffold. However, this compound (which could in theory be converted to |FRAME: CPD-8959| in a single hydroxylation step) was found to be demethylated, and the subsequent hydroxylation is catalyzed by an enzyme with a different stereochemical preference, leading to the formation of |FRAME: CPD-18757| |CITS: [26359402]|. While unexpected, this latter compound is the direct precursor to |FRAME: CPD-16754| |CITS: [1988106]|. Thus, the discovery of the new enzymes opened up the possibility of recombinant biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-16754| and its derivatives instead of the current semisynthetic process.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="chawkins" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
